WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.735
Not only is Jupiter

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:03.937
the largest planet in our solar system,

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.405
it's now also the planet

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.240
with the most moons.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.776
Astronomers discover 12 more moons

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.311
orbiting the gas giant

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.245
using a high powered

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:15.181
telescope in Hawaii about 20 months ago.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.384
And that brings the total number of moons

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:20.553
orbiting Jupiter to 92.

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:23.556
Saturn has the second most moons with 83,

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:26.192
followed by Uranus and Neptune.

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:29.295
And in comparison, Mercury and Venus

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.964
both have zero.

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.832
Joining us right now is Adam Frank.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:33.533
He's a professor

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.301
of astrophysics at the University

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.936
of Rochester in New York.

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:37.971
He's also the author

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.572
of Light of the Stars

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.242
Alien Worlds and the Fate of Our Earth.

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:45.045
Professor Frank, so good to see you.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:47.614
Good to see you again.

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:48.214
Great.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:50.683
So what can we learn from these findings

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.354
Well, the main thing that you learn from

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:55.922
this is that Jupiter, which is,

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:58.158
you know, the king of the solar system,

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.027
has this enormous capacity

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:03.530
just to Hoover in everything. Right.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.231
Jupiter is

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:08.001
a giant vacuum cleaner for space debris,

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.235
which is really important for us

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.804
because we might not be here

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:12.005
if it wasn't for Jupiter

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:13.640
acting kind of like the big brother

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.874
of the solar system

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.242
and clearing material out

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.877
that normally might have pummeled

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:20.580
the Earth and sterilized the big bag.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.815
More evidence of that role

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:23.450
that Jupiter plays. Right.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.452
So so NASA's now is expected

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:26.853
to launch a mission

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:27.821
next year

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:29.556
that will visit Jupiter

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.391
and its moon, Europa.

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:33.760
Tell us a little bit about Europa.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.663
What makes this moon so important?

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:39.833
Yeah, Europa is insane.

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:40.700
I mean it into solar.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.435
And we didn't know this like a while back

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.503
when I was a kid.

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.572
Europa has an ocean.

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:47.107
Europa actually has twice

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.943
the amount of water that Earth does.

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:52.078
So its ocean is larger.

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.147
It's 100 miles deep

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:56.216
and it's covered by a ten mile

00:01:56.216 --> 00:01:58.318
thick layer of ice.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:00.653
So who knows what is down there?

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:02.722
There could be an entire aquatic

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:05.992
ecosystem there.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:06.259
So the

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.461
discovery that we had these ocean moons

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.530
and there's others around the large,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.831
large planets as well,

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.700
really was groundbreaking

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.801
in terms of understanding

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.569
whether there's other places for life

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:17.637
in the solar system.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.339
Forget the universe, just in the solar.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.641
So with that much water frozen

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:22.408
or otherwise,

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:23.042
it's it's

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.210
almost certain in your view

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.346
that there would be some forms of life.

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.149
Well, I wouldn't go that far.

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.016
And scientists

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:32.152
have to be very conservative sorry.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:34.687
But it's the thing that's really cool is

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.289
the Jupiter is so big,

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:38.424
its gravity is constantly twisting

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.293
the insides of Europa,

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.328
turning it into kind of warm taffy.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:43.863
And so then at the bottom of that

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:45.932
100 mile deep ocean,

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:47.433
you could have thermal vents

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.268
where there's all kinds

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:50.370
of chemical shenanigans going on.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:51.604
And that's the kind of place

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.072
where you might expect life to start.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.742
We think life started that way on Earth.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.010
So but the problem for us

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:57.844
is how do you get through

00:02:57.844 --> 00:02:59.245
six miles of ice? Right.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.480
I mean, that's a that's a pretty.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.681
Even the guys in Minnesota

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.216
would have a hard time getting through.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:04.083
How about that?

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.952
So we probably would know

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.253
how to probe down there.

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.288
But it certainly is

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.790
it is one of the most profound mysteries

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:12.058
in the solar system.

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:13.126
So pretty cool.

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:14.127
Yeah, it is.

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.061
I like shenanigans.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.429
I like the way you put it.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.197
So elsewhere

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:20.400
in outer space of NASA's curiosity rover

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.402
found some of the clearest evidence

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:26.005
yet of an ancient lake on Mars.

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:26.739
And scientists

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:27.807
say it's the best evidence

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:28.908
of water and waves

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:31.211
they've seen this entire mission.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:33.479
So why is this significant?

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.848
And is how promising might it be?

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:38.017
Can we just have

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:38.551
a moment

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.252
before we do anything else

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:41.321
to freak out about that picture?

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:44.991
Those are like 4 billion year old waves.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:45.992
Those are like when you walk,

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:46.893
it is amazing.

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:48.995
And you see the ripple right

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:51.030
and this is another planet.

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:51.931
And these are

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:54.033
those are 4 billion years old.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.535
I mean, there weren't even dinosaurs

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:56.236
here yet.

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:57.337
So it's okay now.

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.504
I've got to get past that.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:03:59.205
I like this.

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.908
But these are my nightmares about this

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:03.009
is that it's

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.844
actually really concrete evidence.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:05.578
We've been building up

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:06.813
evidence that Mars,

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:09.115
which right now is a frozen desert,

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:12.151
once had oceans, or at least huge

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:13.319
lakes with water

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.989
rippling up on the surface.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:18.157
And this is the perfect place

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.492
for life to get started.

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:21.227
So this is really clear evidence

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:22.028
that Mars,

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.062
first of all, underwent

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.597
some pretty dramatic climate change.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.633
You want to talk about climate change,

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.134
but that also more

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:30.136
is still under the ground in Mars,

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.872
that life has managed to hold on.

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.507
Maybe there are microbes just, you know,

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:37.010
a couple of meters below that surface

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:38.278
which are still kind of,

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:39.212
you know, hanging on

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:40.546
and doing their thing. They're still.

